1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:08,500
AI coding agents say they fixed your accessibility bugs. But how do you know?

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:18,500
A11Y Lens is a plugin for Hermes. It opens the page in a real headless Chromium, and runs the axe-core engine against the rendered DOM.

3
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:26,500
Thirty-nine violations. Not guessed — measured. Every finding carries a CSS selector, the HTML, and the reason it fails.

4
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:31,800
Every number in this report is axe-core output from an actual browser run.

5
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:34,300
An empty button. Images with no alt text.

6
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:37,800
Contrast eight times too low. A select with no label.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,600
A11Y Lens never edits your code. The agent repairs the source with its normal file tools.

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,400
Adding labels, alt text, lang, a title. Fixing the role typo.

9
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:55,400
Real contrast values, computed on real colors.

10
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
The same page, after the repair.

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,500
Now the part that matters. Re-open the page. Re-test.

12
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:20,400
Compare. Resolved. Remaining. And newly introduced — regressions the agent cannot hide. Not “trust me”: the rendered page, re-tested.

13
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:25,100
It never claims WCAG compliance.

14
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:28,500
A clean scan is where human testing begins.

15
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:37,500
A11Y Lens. Don't just trust the fix. Verify it.

